Q1. Deadlock Debugging

Cause of Deadlock
•P1 locks R1 and waits for R2.
•P2 locks R2 and waits for R1.
•Each process waits for the resource held by the other (circular wait), causing a deadlock.

Optimization:
Acquire resources in the same order in both processes.

Corrected Pseudo Code
Process P1:
lock(R1)
lock(R2)
critical section
unlock(R2)
unlock(R1)

Process P2:
lock(R1)
lock(R2)
critical section
unlock(R2)
unlock(R1)

Q2. CPU Utilization Problem 

Cause:
•Processes repeatedly move between Ready and Waiting due to frequent I/O operations.
•CPU stays idle while processes wait for I/O, resulting in 30% CPU utilization.

Optimization:
•Use asynchronous I/O or I/O buffering.
•Increase multiprogramming so another process can use the CPU while one waits.

Q3. Memory Bottleneck Debugging 

Cause:
•Frequent page faults due to large heap usage (150 MB) and many threads (25 stacks).
•Excessive memory accesses reduce performance.

Optimizations:
•Reduce memory usage (optimize heap allocation).
•Reduce the number of threads or use a thread pool.

Q4. Starvation Debugging

Cause:
•Process P5 is in Q3 (lowest priority).
•Higher-priority queues (Q1 and Q2) continuously receive processes.
•P5 never gets CPU time (starvation).

Optimization
•Apply Aging (gradually increase the priority of waiting processes).
•Allocate CPU time periodically to lower-priority queues.

Q5. Context-Switch Overhead Debugging

Cause:
•Very small time quantum (1 ms).
•Average burst time is 2 ms, so threads are preempted frequently.
•High I/O activity also causes many context switches.

Optimizations
•Increase the time quantum (e.g., 4–5 ms).
•Reduce unnecessary context switches using I/O batching or improve I/O efficiency.

